@@ -650,6 +652,8 @@ When the server is started with `--readonly` (or `OFF_BY_ONE_READONLY=1`), these
 
 The WebSocket chat endpoint (`/ws/chat`) is also disabled (it would spawn a solve).
 
+On a writable server, `/ws/chat` speaks a small JSON frame protocol (`internal/web/chat.go`): a `system` greeting on connect, then per user message an **immediate `status` frame**, a `status` frame (`"Still working… Ns"`, elapsed seconds) every 10 seconds until the first `agent` frame arrives, the `agent` frame(s) carrying the answer, and `error` frames on failure. The server also sends a WebSocket ping every 15s (half the 30s idle read timeout; the interval tracks `readTimeout/2` with a 1s floor, see `pingLoop` in `internal/web/chat.go`). A client that never answers pings is disconnected as soon as a ping fails — non-browser clients must rely on their WebSocket library's automatic pong handling (most do), or expect the disconnect after roughly a read-timeout of silence.
+
 `POST /api/v1/problems/discover` is deliberately exempt: discovery is a pure read (cached-answer lookup that mutates nothing), so agents can still discover pre-verified answers from a read-only catalog — the same rule `readOnlyAllowedPost` implements in `internal/api/server.go`. `GET` endpoints for problems, taxonomy, stats, answers, and discovery remain available.
 
